.TH MPF-CHECK 1 "2026-11-19" "MPF 3.11.1 (6daa0e50)" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpf-check \- write the Redump submission info for a finished disc dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpf-check
.I system
.B \-u
.I program
.RI [ options ]
.IR path " ..."
.br
.B mpf-check
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mpf-check
(upstream \fBMPF.Check\fR) is the checking program of the Media
Preservation Frontend (MPF). It reads a finished dump and the logs its
dumping program left next to it, and writes the Redump submission
information (\fI!submissionInfo.txt\fR) into the same folder. This is the
step
.BR mpf-cli (1)
and
.BR mpf-gui (1)
run at the end of a dump; with
.B mpf-check
it runs on its own, on a dump made earlier or with another program.
.PP
.I path
is the dump's \fI.cue\fR or \fI.iso\fR, or its \fI_logs.zip\fR; several
paths are checked one after another.
.I system
is a system code such as
.B pc
or
.BR psx ;
.B mpf-check listsystems
prints them all.
.I program
is the program that made the dump (see \fB\-u\fR).
.PP
.B mpf-check
overwrites an existing submission information file and log archive without
asking. Copy them away first if you need them.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR ? ", " h ", " help
Print the help text (but see \fBNOTES\fR).
.TP
.B version
Print the program version.
.TP
.BR lc ", " listcodes
List the comment and content site codes.
.TP
.BR lo ", " listconfig
List the current configuration values.
.TP
.BR lpm ", " listpath
List the path macros (\fB%SYSTEM%\fR, \fB%DATE%\fR, ...).
.TP
.BR lm ", " listmedia
List the media type codes.
.TP
.BR ls ", " listsystems
List the system codes.
.TP
.BR lp ", " listprograms
List the programs whose output MPF reads.
.TP
.BR i ", " interactive
Ask for every setting in a menu instead of taking options.
.TP
.B man
Print a manual page generated from the program's own option list.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option sets its value for this run, over the configured one (see
\fBCONFIGURATION\fR). Where an option comes in a pair, the last one given
wins.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-use " \fIprogram\fR"
The program that made the dump:
.BR redumper ,
.BR aaru ,
.B dic
(DiscImageCreator) or
.BR dreamdump ;
or, for dumps MPF only checks,
.BR cleanrip ,
.BR generic ,
.BR ps3cfw ,
.B uic
(UmdImageCreator),
.B wudd
or
.B xbc
(XboxBackupCreator).
Required until the configuration is in use (see \fBCONFIGURATION\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-load\-seed " file"
Take the user information from this submission JSON.
.TP
.BR \-\-placeholders ", " \-\-no\-placeholders
Fill the fields MPF cannot determine with placeholder text (default), or
leave them empty.
.TP
.BR \-\-retrieve ", " \-\-no\-retrieve
Look the dump up on redump.org (default), or work offline.
.TP
.BR \-\-pull\-all ", " \-\-no\-pull\-all
Also take all the other information redump.org has for a match.
.TP
.BI \-\-attempt\-count " n"
How often to try an online request (default 3).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout\-seconds " n"
How long to wait for an online request (default 30).
.TP
.BR \-\-create\-ird ", " \-\-no\-create\-ird
Create an IRD from the dump (PlayStation 3 Blu-ray discs only).
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path " \fIdirpath\fR"
Where the disc's filesystem is mounted. MPF.Check reads the files there for
the additional checks and for the protection scan.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scan ", " \-\-no\-scan
Scan the files under \fB\-p\fR for copy protection, and write what it finds
to a protection information file.
.TP
.BR \-\-archives ", " \-\-no\-archives
Also scan inside archives (default), or not.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug\-protection ", " \-\-no\-debug\-protection
Add the scanner's debug information to the protection output.
.TP
.BR \-\-hide\-drive\-letters ", " \-\-show\-drive\-letters
Hide the drive letters in the protection output, or show them (default).
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-suffix ", " \-\-no\-suffix
Append the dump's file name to the names of the files MPF writes.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json ", " \-\-no\-json
Also write the submission information as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-\-artifacts ", " \-\-no\-artifacts
Include the artifacts in the JSON.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zip ", " \-\-no\-zip
Pack the logs into \fI<name>_logs.zip\fR (see \fBOLDER SYSTEMS\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-compression " method"
How to pack them:
.BR store ,
.B deflate
or
.B deflatemaximum
(the default). Any other word means
.BR deflate .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete ", " \-\-no\-delete
Delete the files a submission does not need.
.SH CONFIGURATION
MPF reads
.I ~/.config/mpf/config.json
when it exists, and only otherwise a
.I config.json
next to the binary; the file is shared with
.BR mpf-cli (1)
and
.BR mpf-gui (1).
As long as it has no
.B \(dqFirstRun\(dq: \(dqFalse\(dq
entry,
.B mpf-check
ignores it and starts from its own defaults: no dumping program (so
\fB\-u\fR is required), online lookup and placeholders on, protection scan,
JSON, log compression and deletion off. The file the wrapper creates has no
such entry;
.BR mpf-gui (1)
writes it the first time its options window is closed. From then on the
configured values are the defaults, the configured dumping program
included.
.PP
At start
.B mpf-check
asks online for a newer MPF release. It does so before it reads the
configuration, so the
.B CheckForUpdatesOnStartup
setting does not turn that off here.
.SH DRIVE ACCESS
.B mpf-check
sends no commands to a drive and carries no file capabilities. It needs
read access to the dump, write access to its folder, and with \fB\-p\fR
read access to the mounted disc.
.SH OLDER SYSTEMS
MPF reads and writes its log archives (\fI*_logs.zip\fR) through a library
that needs glibc 2.34 or newer. Below that (Enterprise Linux 8 has 2.28),
compressing the logs fails and leaves an empty archive, and extracting an
archive writes every file from it as 0 bytes, over any copy already in the
folder, while MPF still reports success. There the wrapper turns
.B CompressLogFiles
off in the configuration and refuses to start
.B mpf-check
.IP \(bu 2
with \fB\-z\fR or \fB\-\-zip\fR (also written \fB\-z=\fR... or
\fB\-\-zip=\fR...), unless a later \fB\-\-no\-zip\fR turns it off again;
.IP \(bu 2
on a \fI_logs.zip\fR that lists files, or on a path that has one next to
it. Extract such an archive with
.BR unzip ,
move it out of the folder, and check the extracted files.
.PP
The wrapper cannot see what you choose in interactive mode: there leave
"Compress logs" off and give no path with a log archive next to it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write the submission information for a redumper dump of a PC disc,
offline:
.PP
.RS
.B mpf-check pc \-u redumper \-\-no\-retrieve ~/ISO/disc/disc.cue
.RE
.PP
The same with a protection scan of the disc mounted at
.IR /mnt/disc :
.PP
.RS
.B mpf-check pc \-u redumper \-s \-p /mnt/disc ~/ISO/disc/disc.cue
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/mpf-check
The wrapper described above; it starts the binary below.
.TP
.I /usr/lib64/mpf-check/MPF.Check
The self-contained .NET binary.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mpf/config.json
The configuration, shared with
.BR mpf-cli (1)
and
.BR mpf-gui (1).
.SH NOTES
This page is written by hand against MPF 3.11.1 commit 6daa0e50 and lists
the options that build parses. Its help text is older: it shows
\fB\-\-disable\-archives\fR, \fB\-\-enable\-debug\fR and
\fB\-\-include\-artifacts\fR where the program takes \fB\-\-no\-archives\fR,
\fB\-\-debug\-protection\fR and \fB\-\-artifacts\fR, names
\fB\-\-username\fR and \fB\-\-password\fR, which the program does not
take, and says that flags toggle the configured value. A word the program
does not take as an option is checked as a path. MPF is packaged as a
rolling build, so the installed binary may be newer than this page.
